# Issue #149-5: Implement Workflow Persistence & State Recovery (Phase 5)

**Parent Issue:** #149  
**Phase:** 5 of 6  
**Blocked by:** Issue #149-3, #149-4 (workflow execution wired ✅)  
**Blocks:** Phase 6

## Description

Configure Elsa workflow persistence to SQL Server, implement state recovery on application startup, and enable workflow history tracking. This ensures workflows survive application restarts and provides debugging visibility.

## Current State

- ✅ BuildProcessWorkflow defined (Phase 3)
- ✅ GraphQL mutations trigger workflows (Phase 4)
- ❌ Persistence backend not configured
- ❌ State recovery service not implemented
- ❌ Workflow history not tracked

## Requirements

### 5.1 Configure Elsa SQL Server Persistence

**File:** `backend/src/FactoryApp.WebApi/Program.cs` (Elsa registration section, lines 105-117)

Update Elsa configuration to use SQL Server persistence:

```csharp
// Add to builder.Services.AddElsa() chain
builder.Services.AddElsa(options =>
{
    options
        .AddActivitiesFrom<Program>()
        .AddWorkflowsFrom<Program>()

        // NEW: Configure SQL Server persistence
        .UseEntityFrameworkPersistence(options =>
        {
            options.UseSqlServer(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("FactoryDb"));
        })

        // Enable workflow history
        .UseWorkflowHistory()

        // Enable runtime storage (for variables)
        .UseWorkflowRuntimeStorage();
});
```

### 5.2 Create Elsa Schema Migration

**File:** `backend/src/FactoryApp.Workflows/Migrations/InitializeElsaSchema.cs` (NEW)

Create EF Core migration to set up Elsa tables:

```csharp
public partial class InitializeElsaSchema : Migration
{
    protected override void Up(MigrationBuilder migrationBuilder)
    {
        // Elsa creates its own schema automatically via DbContext
        // This migration documents the schema version for tracking
        migrationBuilder.CreateTable(
            name: "ElsaSchema",
            columns: table => new
            {
                Id = table.Column<int>(nullable: false)
                    .Annotation("SqlServer:Identity", "1, 1"),
                Version = table.Column<string>(nullable: false),
                CreatedAt = table.Column<DateTime>(nullable: false)
            },
            constraints: table =>
            {
                table.PrimaryKey("PK_ElsaSchema", x => x.Id);
            });

        migrationBuilder.InsertData(
            table: "ElsaSchema",
            columns: new[] { "Version", "CreatedAt" },
            values: new object[] { "3.6.0", DateTime.UtcNow });
    }

    protected override void Down(MigrationBuilder migrationBuilder)
    {
        migrationBuilder.DropTable(name: "ElsaSchema");
    }
}
```

### 5.3 Implement WorkflowRecoveryService

**File:** `backend/src/FactoryApp.Services/WorkflowRecoveryService.cs` (NEW)

Service to resume incomplete workflows on startup:

```csharp
public class WorkflowRecoveryService : BackgroundService
{
    private readonly IServiceProvider _serviceProvider;
    private readonly ILogger<WorkflowRecoveryService> _logger;
    private readonly FactoryDbContext _dbContext;
    private readonly IWorkflowHost _workflowHost;

    public WorkflowRecoveryService(
        IServiceProvider serviceProvider,
        ILogger<WorkflowRecoveryService> logger,
        FactoryDbContext dbContext,
        IWorkflowHost workflowHost)
    {
        _serviceProvider = serviceProvider;
        _logger = logger;
        _dbContext = dbContext;
        _workflowHost = workflowHost;
    }

    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        // Run recovery after app startup (delay 5 seconds)
        await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5), stoppingToken);

        try
        {
            _logger.LogInformation("Starting workflow recovery service");

            // Find all running workflows in persistence store
            var runningWorkflows = await _dbContext.WorkflowInstances
                .Where(w => w.Status == WorkflowStatus.Running)
                .ToListAsync(stoppingToken);

            _logger.LogInformation("Found {Count} incomplete workflows to recover", runningWorkflows.Count);

            foreach (var workflow in runningWorkflows)
            {
                try
                {
                    // Resume workflow from saved state
                    var result = await _workflowHost.ResumeAsync(
                        workflowInstanceId: workflow.Id,
                        cancellationToken: stoppingToken);

                    _logger.LogInformation(
                        "Resumed workflow {WorkflowId} (status: {Status})",
                        workflow.Id, result.Status);
                }
                catch (Exception ex)
                {
                    _logger.LogError(ex,
                        "Failed to resume workflow {WorkflowId}",
                        workflow.Id);
                }
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _logger.LogError(ex, "Workflow recovery service failed");
        }
    }
}
```

